The '''GA144''' is an [[asynchronous circuit|asynchronous]], 144-core [[microprocessor]] designed by [[Charles H. Moore]] and marketed by GreenArrays.{{cite web |last=Windeck |first=Christof |title=Sparsamer Forth-Mikrocontroller mit 144 Kernen |trans-title=Frugal Forth microcontroller with 144 cores |language=de |website=Heise online |date=20 October 2010 |url=https://www.heise.de/news/Sparsamer-Forth-Mikrocontroller-mit-144-Kernen-1122006.html |access-date=17 September 2026}} It is a spatial processor: its 144 independent 18-bit, stack-based F18A computers are arranged in an 18-by-8 array and communicate with adjacent nodes rather than through a shared memory.{{cite thesis |last=Phothilimthana |first=Phitchaya Mangpo |title=Chlorophyll: Synthesis-Aided Compiler for Low-Power Spatial Architectures |type=Master's report |publisher=University of California, Berkeley |date=15 May 2015 |url=https://digicoll.lib.berkeley.edu/record/139996/files/EECS-2015-121.pdf |pages=3–4 |access-date=17 September 2026}} The device was sold as the GA144-1.2 / G144A12 and was announced for sale by GreenArrays in October 2011.{{cite web |title=News |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/news/index.php |website=GreenArrays |date=4 October 2011 |access-date=17 September 2026}}

==Architecture==

Each F18A node has local RAM and ROM and executes independently, without a global clock or shared memory.{{cite thesis |last=Phothilimthana |first=Phitchaya Mangpo |title=Chlorophyll: Synthesis-Aided Compiler for Low-Power Spatial Architectures |type=Master's report |publisher=University of California, Berkeley |date=15 May 2015 |pages=3–4 |url=https://digicoll.lib.berkeley.edu/record/139996/files/EECS-2015-121.pdf |access-date=17 September 2026}} The nodes use blocking reads and writes to communicate with neighbouring nodes; consequently, programs that span distant nodes must explicitly route messages and avoid deadlock.{{cite thesis |last=Phothilimthana |first=Phitchaya Mangpo |title=Chlorophyll: Synthesis-Aided Compiler for Low-Power Spatial Architectures |type=Master's report |publisher=University of California, Berkeley |date=15 May 2015 |page=4 |url=https://digicoll.lib.berkeley.edu/record/139996/files/EECS-2015-121.pdf |access-date=17 September 2026}}

The F18A is an 18-bit stack architecture. A node has 64 words each of RAM and ROM; its small local memories and stacks require programs and data to be divided among nodes for larger tasks.{{cite web |last=Back |first=Andrew |title=Hands on with a 144 core processor |website=DesignSpark |date=16 July 2012 |url=https://www.rs-online.com/designspark/hands-on-with-a-144-core-processor |access-date=17 September 2026}}{{cite thesis |last=Phothilimthana |first=Phitchaya Mangpo |title=Chlorophyll: Synthesis-Aided Compiler for Low-Power Spatial Architectures |type=Master's report |publisher=University of California, Berkeley |date=15 May 2015 |page=4 |url=https://digicoll.lib.berkeley.edu/record/139996/files/EECS-2015-121.pdf |access-date=17 September 2026}} GreenArrays supplied ''arrayForth'', a Forth-based development environment, for creating code for the nodes.{{cite web |title=EVB001 Evaluation Board for G144A12 |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/documents/greg/EVB001-144x2-Rev1.pdf |publisher=GreenArrays |pages=5–6 |access-date=17 September 2026}}

==Input and output==

Twenty-two edge nodes have one or more external pins. The interfaces include general-purpose digital I/O, five analogue input/output pairs, two 18-bit parallel interfaces, SPI, serial boot interfaces, and two serializer/deserializer interfaces.{{cite web |title=GA144-1.10 product brief |url=https://greenarraychip.com/home/documents/greg/PB001-100503-GA144-1-10.pdf |publisher=GreenArrays |access-date=17 September 2026}} The chip is supplied in an 88-pin QFN package.{{cite web |last=Back |first=Andrew |title=Hands on with a 144 core processor |website=DesignSpark |date=16 July 2012 |url=https://www.rs-online.com/designspark/hands-on-with-a-144-core-processor |access-date=17 September 2026}}

==Performance and power==

GreenArrays specified a peak aggregate rate of 96 billion operations per second and a power range of 14 microwatts to 650 milliwatts, dependent on activity.{{cite web |title=G144A12 Chip Reference Data Book |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/documents/greg/DB002-110705-G144A12.pdf |publisher=GreenArrays |date=5 July 2011 |access-date=17 September 2026}} Because the device is asynchronous, its timing and energy use vary with node activity, supply voltage, temperature, and the instruction mix.{{cite web |title=G144A12 Chip Reference Data Book |url=https://www.greenarraychips.com/home/documents/greg/DB002-110705-G144A12.pdf |publisher=GreenArrays |date=5 July 2011 |access-date=17 September 2026}} A Berkeley evaluation characterized the GA144 as an extremely minimalist low-power spatial architecture, while noting that its programming model imposes substantial layout and partitioning work.{{cite thesis |last=Phothilimthana |first=Phitchaya Mangpo |title=Chlorophyll: Synthesis-Aided Compiler for Low-Power Spatial Architectures |type=Master's report |publisher=University of California, Berkeley |date=15 May 2015 |pages=2–4 |url=https://digicoll.lib.berkeley.edu/record/139996/files/EECS-2015-121.pdf |access-date=17 September 2026}}

